  <p>"Brand's call" means no statute read for this page adds a label to that image; casting and setting there are production decisions, covered further down. Every cell was checked against the published texts on Oct. 10, 2026.</p>

  <h2>Which rules apply in all four markets?</h2>

  <p>Two EU directives sit under every version of the campaign: one for the price, one for the green line.</p>

  <p>The price rule is the older one. It has applied since May 2022.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Any announcement of a price reduction must show the prior price, the lowest price the trader charged in a period of at least 30 days before the reduction.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Directive (EU) 2019/2161, Article 6a of Directive 98/6/EC, applied from May 28, 2022. Instrument text, read October 10, 2026.</p>

  <p>All four markets wrote the 30 days into their own law: France in Article L112-1-1 of its Consumer Code, Germany in section 11 of its Price Indication Ordinance, Spain in Article 20 of its Retail Trade Act and Italy in Article 17-bis of its Consumer Code. For production, that turns the "was" price into a data question. The artwork can be finished early. The number in it waits for whoever owns the price history, in each market separately.</p>

  <p>The green rule is newer, and it bites harder.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">From Sept. 27, 2026, a generic environmental claim the trader cannot back with recognized excellent environmental performance is banned outright, along with sustainability labels that rest on no certification scheme or public authority and climate-neutral claims based on offsetting.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Directive (EU) 2024/825, Article 4 and the points it adds to Annex I of Directive 2005/29/EC, February 28, 2024. Instrument text, read October 10, 2026.</p>

  <p>The directive spells out what "generic" means.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The directive's own examples of generic claims include "eco-friendly," "green," "nature's friend," "climate friendly" and "biodegradable." A claim stops being generic when its specification sits, clear and prominent, on the same medium, such as the same ad spot.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Directive (EU) 2024/825, recital 9, February 28, 2024. Instrument text, read October 10, 2026.</p>

  <p>So "eco-friendly packaging" goes. A line that says something true and provable, on the same frame, can stay in. The recital gives its own pair: "climate-friendly packaging" is generic, while a statement that all the energy used to make the packaging came from renewable sources is specific.</p>

  <p>A directive binds governments, not shops. The four markets are not at the same point.</p>

