  <p>One master serves all four, so build it to the strictest of them, which today means the German and Italian texts.</p>

  <p>Don't confuse this text with the Green Claims Directive. That is a separate proposal from 2023, and the European Parliament's legislative tracker listed it as blocked in September 2026.</p>

  <h2>What does France add?</h2>

  <p>Three rules, each touching something the viewer sees.</p>

  <p><strong>Language.</strong> The Toubon law of 1994 makes French obligatory in the offer and description of goods and services. Its Article 2 adds: "Les mêmes dispositions s'appliquent à toute publicité écrite, parlée ou audiovisuelle." English can run, but a French version has to sit beside it, and Article 4 requires the French to be as legible, audible or intelligible as the foreign text. Putting an English slogan into a registered trademark doesn't get around it. The same Article 2 says so.</p>

  <p><strong>Retouched photos.</strong> A commercial photo of a model whose body was reshaped with software carries a label.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"Photographie retouchée" must accompany a commercial photo of a model whose silhouette was slimmed or thickened with image software, on posters, online, in the press and in printed advertising. The fine is 37,500 euros, or up to 30 percent of the advertising spend.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: French Public Health Code, Article L2133-2, and Decree 2017-738, in force since October 1, 2017. Statute text on Legifrance, read October 10, 2026.</p>

  <p>The decree puts the check on the advertiser, which must find out whether the photos it buys were altered. If the master was retouched for shape, the French file gets the label, legible and set apart from the message.</p>

  <p><strong>Influencer posts.</strong> Content that paid influencers publish has its own labels, and they are plural.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Paid influencers must label images with a reshaped silhouette or face "Images retouchées" and images made by AI to depict a face or a silhouette "Images virtuelles." A breach carries up to a year in prison and a 4,500 euro fine.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: French Law 2023-451 of June 9, 2023, Article 5, as rewritten by Ordinance 2024-978 of November 6, 2024. Statute text on Legifrance, read October 10, 2026.</p>

  <p>That reaches the creator cut of the campaign, not just the brand's own ads. A generated face in a sponsored post is "Images virtuelles" in France, whatever the rest of Europe asks.</p>

  <h2>What does Germany ask of the price card?</h2>

  <p>Mostly, how the price is shown.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Every announced price reduction in Germany must state the lowest total price charged to consumers in the 30 days before it, and any price per weight, volume, length or area needs the unit price beside the total.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: German Price Indication Ordinance (PAngV) of November 12, 2021, sections 4, 5 and 11, as amended May 12, 2026. Statute text, read October 10, 2026.</p>

  <p>The unit is one kilogram, one liter, one meter, one square meter or one cubic meter. A price card built for another market, showing a 400 ml bottle at its total price alone, fails here.</p>

  <p>The Unfair Competition Act adds a duty not to leave things out. Section 5a treats withholding essential information as misleading, and section 5b lists what counts in an offer: the main characteristics of the product, the trader's identity and address, and the total price. A short cut that drops the price line to save a second is an easy way to break it.</p>

  <p>Germany wrote the green rules into national law by an act of February 2026. It has no legal sale season at all. The 2004 rewrite of that act abolished the old end-of-season sales rules, calendar and product range alike.</p>

  <h2>What do Spain and Italy add?</h2>

  <p>Spain's General Advertising Act of 1988 makes misleading, unfair and aggressive advertising unlawful and treats it as unfair competition. Its consumer law wants mandatory label information at least in Castilian. That is a labeling rule, not an advertising one, but it shapes the shot: if a Spanish-market pack exists, it is the pack to show. Sales are the trader's call.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Spanish retailers choose their own sale seasons and decide how long each runs.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Spain's Retail Trade Act, Ley 7/1996, Article 25, as amended in 2012. Consolidated text on the Official State Gazette, read October 10, 2026.</p>

  <p>Italy's Consumer Code carries its own language rule. Article 9 requires all consumer information at least in Italian, and where other languages appear, the Italian can't be less visible or legible. Foreign words in common use are allowed. Article 21 now names environmental characteristics among the things an ad can mislead about.</p>

  <p>Italian sale dates are set region by region, on a shared rule.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Italy's regions start winter sales on the first working day before Epiphany and summer sales on the first Saturday of July, under a common rule agreed in 2011.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Tuscany regional resolution 607 of May 18, 2026, and Lombardy's sales page, updated May 27, 2026, both read October 10, 2026. Regional authorities restating the rule.</p>

