  <blockquote class="evidence">French winter sales start the second Wednesday of January at 8 a.m., moved to the first Wednesday when the second falls after the 12th, and run four weeks.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: French ministerial order of May 27, 2019, under Commercial Code Article L310-3. Statute text on Legifrance, read October 10, 2026.</p>

  <p>France had not published its official 2027 notice on Oct. 10, 2026, so the French dates above are the rule applied to the calendar. Two more French details shape the brief. Goods sold as "soldes" must have been on sale and paid for at least a month before the period opens. And the 30-day rule still applies: for a Jan. 6 sale, the reference price is the lowest one charged since early December, holiday promotions included.</p>

  <p>Some Italian areas run their own dates. The Bolzano chamber of commerce listed Jan. 8 for most of Alto Adige in 2026, and March 7 in its tourist towns.</p>

  <h2>What gets reshot, what gets regenerated and what stays?</h2>

  <p>The law decides some of it. Copy, price blocks, the green line and the labels change because a text says so. Casting, setting and props are left to the brand. They are production decisions.</p>

  <p>Split the master into layers before anyone shoots or generates. The product, the light and the framing are shared: they are the hardest part to get right, and nothing in the four markets' rules asks for them to change. The people, the setting, the props and anything with words on it are candidates for a market version.</p>

  <p>Then decide layer by layer. Would a buyer in that market recognize the street or the kitchen in the frame? Is there a legal reason to change it, a pack or a label? Is it cheaper to regenerate a background than to reshoot a scene? A generated setting behind the real product is often the cheapest local version there is.</p>

  <p>A generated person is not a free swap. In France it can trigger "Images virtuelles" in a creator's post, and in all four markets a realistic synthetic person can be a deep fake.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/resources/insights/campaign-adaptation-cost-per-market">campaign adaptation cost per market</a> piece prices these levels of change.</p>

  <h2>Do formats change from one country to another?</h2>

  <p>No. On the same network, a French, German, Spanish and Italian ad use the same shapes.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Meta publishes one set of ad specs with no country selector: a feed image at 4:5 and 1440 x 1800 pixels, and a Reels video at 9:16 and 1440 x 2560, with 14 percent of the top, 35 percent of the bottom and 6 percent of each side kept free of text and logos.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Ads Guide, Facebook Feed image and Instagram Reels video pages, read October 10, 2026. Platform specification pages.</p>

  <p>TikTok's only regional split in its in-feed specs is a separate safe-zone file for right-to-left scripts. LinkedIn's single-image sizes carry no country variant either. The master stays one set of shapes, and nobody should be rebuilding formats per market.</p>

  <p>What does change on the platform is language matching. LinkedIn's help center says "Ad creative and landing page language should match the audience language selected in ad set audience settings," and the language can't be changed once the ad set is live. TikTok's review checklist wants captions, spoken words and subtitles to match a language accepted in every country an ad group targets. In practice: one ad set or ad group per market language.</p>

  <h2>Do AI-generated visuals need a label in these markets?</h2>

  <p>Since Aug. 2, 2026, Article 50 of the EU AI Act asks the brand or agency that deploys a deep fake to disclose it. The Commission's guidelines count a realistic synthetic person in an ad as a deep fake, and an AI image that misleads about how a product looks or works. A real product against a generated background is not one, as long as the ad doesn't mislead. The <a href="/resources/insights/eu-ai-act-labeling-brand-content">EU AI Act labeling piece</a> covers who applies the label and how the platform labels line up with it. For creator content, France adds its own label on top.</p>

  <h2>How do you keep one master and four versions in order?</h2>

  <p>Keep every version of the launch in one place. Send each market's cut to its own reviewer.</p>

  <p>In the hubStudio app, <a href="/app/campaigns">Campaigns</a> keep one launch's files under one name with a brief: the renders, the edited videos, the posts and the documents. A campaign points at files in the Assets Library and copies nothing, so the French cut and the master stay side by side, and a new version stays in. Validation then sends each market's version to its reviewer, a teammate or a client, who approves it or sends it back with a comment. Versions stay on one thread. A reviewer in Paris can run the app in French.</p>

  <p><strong>A change list for the brief.</strong> It can be pasted as it stands.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Copy: transcreate per market; in France, French beside any English, at the same legibility.</li>
    <li>Price: the lowest price of the last 30 days per market, from the price owner, not from design; in Germany, the unit price beside any price by quantity.</li>
    <li>Green line: cut every generic claim; keep only a specific, provable claim stated on the same frame.</li>
    <li>Images: log which files were reshaped; "Photographie retouchée" on French ads; "Images retouchées" or "Images virtuelles" on French creator posts.</li>
    <li>People and settings: no rule decides these, so the brief has to.</li>
    <li>Formats stay as they are. One set of shapes covers all four markets.</li>
    <li>Dates: French soldes from Jan. 6, 2027; Italy by region; Germany and Spain at the retailer's call.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The <a href="/resources/insights/transcreation-as-a-production-line">transcreation as a production line</a> piece covers the copy side of this list, and the <a href="/services/design/ad-creative">ad creative design service</a> builds the per-market batch. The <a href="/solutions/platforms/meta">Meta platform page</a> covers the network itself.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about localizing a campaign for Europe</h2>

  <h3>What changes when you localize an ad campaign for Europe?</h3>

  <p>More than the words. In France, Germany, Spain and Italy a discount must show the lowest price of the last 30 days, generic green claims are banned under an EU directive applied from Sept. 27, 2026, France adds language and retouching rules, and sale dates follow national or regional rules. Formats stay the same on each network.</p>

  <h3>Is it legal to run an English-only ad in France?</h3>

  <p>Not under the Toubon law. Its Article 2 makes French obligatory in all written, spoken and audiovisual advertising for goods and services. English can appear alongside, but the French must be as legible, audible or intelligible. Products with a foreign name widely known to the public are exempt, and a registered trademark does not exempt a slogan.</p>

  <h3>What is the 30-day prior price rule for discounts in the EU?</h3>

  <p>When a shop announces a price reduction, it must show its prior price, defined as the lowest price it charged in at least the 30 days before the reduction. The rule comes from a 2019 directive applied since May 28, 2022. France, Germany, Spain and Italy all use the 30-day period in their own laws.</p>

  <h3>Can I still say a product is eco-friendly in EU ads in 2026?</h3>

  <p>Not as a bare claim. From Sept. 27, 2026, "eco-friendly," "green" and similar generic claims are banned unless the trader can show recognized excellent environmental performance. A specific claim stated clearly on the same medium can run. Germany and Italy apply the rule in national law; France and Spain had not passed theirs on Oct. 10, 2026.</p>

  <h3>Do I need a retouched photo label in France?</h3>

  <p>Yes, if a model's silhouette in a commercial photo was slimmed or thickened with software. The label reads "Photographie retouchée" and applies to posters, online ads, the press and printed advertising. Paid influencers have a separate duty: "Images retouchées" for a reshaped body or face, and "Images virtuelles" for an AI-generated face or silhouette.</p>

  <h3>When are the official sales periods in France, Spain and Italy?</h3>

  <p>France fixes two four-week periods by ministerial order; the rule puts winter 2027 at Jan. 6 to Feb. 2, pending the official notice. Spain lets each retailer choose its seasons and their length. Italy's regions set the dates on a common rule, which gives Jan. 5, 2027, for winter and July 3 for summer. Germany has no legal sale periods.</p>

  <h3>Do social media formats change from one European country to another?</h3>

  <p>No. Meta, TikTok and LinkedIn publish one set of ad specs for every market, with no country variant, so a French and a German ad use the same shapes. What changes is language: LinkedIn and TikTok want an ad's language to match the audience language of the ad set, so plan one ad set per market language.</p>

